WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:03.410
Horror films are supposed to get your adrenaline
pumping.

00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:05.910
But for some, these thrills are just too much
to handle.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:09.580
It may sound like a clever bit of marketing,
but physical reactions to these movies have

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:13.920
indeed required medical professionals to treat
moviegoers at the hospital, or the scene of

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:14.920
the scare.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:19.160
Here are a few films with fictional frights
intense enough to cause real-life physical

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:20.250
trauma.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:21.529
The Exorcist

00:00:21.529 --> 00:00:25.119
Let's start with a classic: If you've never
seen The Exorcist, then you've probably never

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:26.999
known true cinematic horror.

00:00:26.999 --> 00:00:31.369
Still shocking by even today's sensibilities,
the film had quite an effect on people who

00:00:31.369 --> 00:00:33.050
first saw it in 1973.

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:37.280
Local news reports from the time mention people
crying, throwing up, and even fainting …

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:41.361
"We have a lot of people throwing up and a
lot of people shuddering, but the thing that

00:00:41.361 --> 00:00:43.140
really surprises me is people faint.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.940
I mean, I've never in my life known a movie
where people would faint."

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:50.560
The Exorcist may have been the first, but
it certainly wasn't the last.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:51.760
For example ...

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:52.920
Goodnight Mommy

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:57.370
An Austrian film about 10-year-old twin boys
whose mother has just had facial reconstructive

00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:59.360
surgery doesn't sound all that scary.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:03.980
But once Mommy comes home, all bandaged up,
she seems to be a wholly different person,

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:05.909
and exudes a cold, severe aura.

00:01:05.909 --> 00:01:10.670
While 2015's Goodnight Mommy is mostly a psychological
horror, the latter half of the film is full

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.020
of violence — and some people can't handle
it.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.670
In an interview with IndieWire, the director
says early screenings proved the filmmakers

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:19.600
were onto something, saying, quote, "Two people
fainted.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.590
That's the best compliment we've had so far."

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:23.590
Bite

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:26.439
Have you ever heard the one about the woman
who turned into an insect after getting bit

00:01:26.439 --> 00:01:27.439
by one?

00:01:27.439 --> 00:01:31.960
Well, that's the premise of the 2015 body-horror
movie Bite, and the impact it had on movie-goers

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:33.049
is no joke.

00:01:33.049 --> 00:01:37.210
At the Fantasia International Film Festival
premiere, audience members were given barf

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:40.609
bags as a gag — but some people reportedly
had to make use of them.

00:01:40.609 --> 00:01:44.432
That's not all: First to Know reports a few
people even passed out in the theater, and

00:01:44.432 --> 00:01:47.350
at least one ambulance was called to the scene.

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:48.350
Saw III

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:52.459
By the time we got around to the third Saw
in 2006, you'd have thought fans would have

00:01:52.459 --> 00:01:56.869
built up a tolerance to all of the gross,
violent images from the first two films.

00:01:56.869 --> 00:02:02.100
Obviously this was not the case, as the BBC
reported that moviegoers in a few UK theaters

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:06.009
fainted and emergency services were called
out three times in one night because of the

00:02:06.009 --> 00:02:07.009
film.

00:02:07.009 --> 00:02:10.750
A woman in her 20s even needed to be hospitalized
after apparently fainting with fright, while

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:13.000
others were treated at the scene.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Raw

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.260
The 2016 film Raw premiered at the
Toronto International Film Festival, and wasted

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:21.430
no time in making filmgoers faint and vomit.

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:24.739
The Guardian reported that paramedics had
to be called in because the realistic biting

00:02:24.739 --> 00:02:29.099
and chewing of human flesh, plus all the gory
lacerations and wounds, were all too much

00:02:29.099 --> 00:02:32.099
for some audience members, who fainted in
their seats.

00:02:32.099 --> 00:02:35.400
So ... not a good choice for "dinner and a
movie."

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:36.629
The Green Inferno

00:02:36.629 --> 00:02:41.349
Another gross movie capable of getting
folks to faint is Eli Roth's The Green Inferno,

00:02:41.349 --> 00:02:45.459
which features a group of activists who crash
land in a jungle and have to survive captivity

00:02:45.459 --> 00:02:47.250
at the hands of a hungry tribe.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:51.360
Par for the course for an Eli Roth movie,
The Green Inferno is full of graphic images,

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.299
gratuitous gore, and body mutilation.

00:02:53.299 --> 00:02:56.560
Oh, and of course all the people-eating.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.090
Can't forget about the people-eating!

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:01.840
An article on New Zealand's Newshub said a
woman fainted during a screening in France,

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:06.500
and Roth shared the news on Instagram, clearly
thrilled by the glowing review.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:08.360
The Human Centipede 2

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:12.250
Movieline's Jen Yamato wrote about her experience
at the premiere of The Human Centipede 2 at

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:17.371
Fantastic Fest in 2011 and how festival founder
Tim League joked about there being EMTs on

00:03:17.371 --> 00:03:19.790
hand in case people got sick.

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:23.470
Like the barf bag gag at the Bite premiere,
this joke ended up being less funny when it

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:27.959
came true: the person sitting next to Yamato
filled two barf bags and eventually fainted,

00:03:27.959 --> 00:03:31.879
and two ambulances were called to the scene
to tend to audience members.

00:03:31.879 --> 00:03:33.200
V/H/S

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:38.120
An anthology of scary stories that debuted
at Sundance, 2012's V/H/S certainly had an

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:39.980
effect on viewers at the festival.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:43.689
The first night, a woman left the theater
in tears because she reportedly couldn't stand

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:45.219
the tension and suspense.

00:03:45.219 --> 00:03:48.849
And according to co-writer Simon Barrett,
paramedics had to treat two audience members

00:03:48.849 --> 00:03:52.060
at a midnight screening who suffered seizures
and started vomiting.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:55.930
That sounds awful, but the good news is that
these two troopers decided to go back and

00:03:55.930 --> 00:04:00.530
finish the film anyway, which is just about
the best review a horror flick can get.

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:04.160
"If you're watching this, don't come here!"

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:05.249
Thanks for watching!

00:04:05.249 --> 00:04:08.020
